# Secrets first, and deliberately at the top. I already leaked backend/.env to
# GitHub once on this project by typing the ignore rule into the wrong file, so
# now the rule goes in before the file it protects ever exists. I verify with
# `git check-ignore -v ml/.env` rather than trusting a clean `git status` — a
# tracked file stays quiet in status, which is exactly how I got caught last time.
.env

# I don't commit the venv or the data snapshot — both are regenerable, and the
# CSV is a copy of my database rather than source I maintain.
.venv/
data/*.csv
__pycache__/
.pytest_cache/

# I DO commit the trained pickles (see models/README.md for why).
